  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nHeavy rain has come to an end across the CWA early this morning. An \narea of training thunderstorms developed just north of I-90 in \nWaseca/Steele/Freeborn counties Friday evening and dumped some \nimpressive rainfall totals of 5-7\". But overall, southern and \nwestern Minnesota received 1-3\" with amounts quickly tapering \noff to the north through the Twin Cities metro to only around \n0.25-0.5\" along and north of I-94. Overcast skies and areas of \nfog and drizzle are expected for most of the day as a slow \nmoving surface low in Iowa continues to make its way eastward. \nAs it does, winds are expected to turn northeasterly by early \nthis afternoon, which should slowly begin to filter in some \ndrier air by Sunday. However, extensive cloud cover will persist\nmaking for a fairly gloomy Fall weekend. Temperature wise, \ntoday will be similar to yesterday with maybe a 5 degree change \nfrom overnight lows to daytime highs. \n\nBy Monday, Canadian high pressure takes hold of the Great Lakes and \nwe see our moisture really drop off through mid week. Prime \nradiational cooling conditions set up Monday and Tuesday night, \nwhich will allow lows to fall into the 40s and upper 30s for \nportions of east central Minnesota and western Wisconsin. Some frost \nis possible in outlying areas (particularly western Wisconsin),\nso this will be something we monitor closely heading into next \nweek. There's not much else to be concerned about until the \naforementioned high slides off to the east late Wednesday. \nSoutherly flow starts to come back ahead of our next potential \nsystem on Thursday. Global deterministic models are in fairly \ngood agreement with the timing/placement of a trough moving out \nof the Dakotas late in the week. However, given the very dry \nairmass that we are expecting ahead of this feature, the \nmajority of the precip may end up being confined to our west. \nNBM PoPs are likely too low for Wednesday night/Thursday, but at\nthis point there's still going to be a lot of variability. \nHighs creep back up into the low 70s by the end of the period \nwith ensembles starting to hint at our next precip chance late \nnext weekend.",
